idth":[],"x_height":[]},"description":null,"foundry":"That That Type","is_personal":false,"lastModified":1765929086144,"name":"Cesso","variations":["n4"],"web_link":"http://fonts.adobe.com/fonts/cesso"},{"id":"ysdr","__v":0,"browse_info":{"capitals":["all-or-small-caps"],"classification":["decorative"],"contrast":[],"language":["ca","de","en","es","fr","it","nl","pt","sv"],"number_style":["uppercase"],"recommended_for":[],"weight":["regular"],"width":[],"x_height":[]},"description":"What happens when computer printer marries a chainsaw and a player piano? A piece of 1960s computer equipment called a chain printer that printed at alarming speeds, even by today’s standards. Band printers were similar to chain printers except were more like bandsaws than chainsaws. Most print chains and print bands had limited punctuation, no accents and no lowercase letters. Chainprinter recreates the effect of this mechanical marvel/nightmare of yesteryear. Want a crisp version with lowercase? Try Typodermic’s Linefeed.","foundry":"Typodermic","lastModified":1588840819566,"name":"Chainprinter","variations":["n4"],"web_link":"http://typekit.com/fonts/chainprinter"},{"id":"sgqs","__v":0,"browse_info":{"capitals":[],"classification":[],"contrast":[],"language":["ca","de","en","es","fr","it","nl","pt","sv"],"number_style":[],"recommended_for":[],"weight":[],"width":[],"x_height":[]},"description":null,"foundry":"Alan Meeks","lastModified":1588840819553,"name":"Chalfont","variations":["n3","i3","n5","i5","n7","i7","n8"],"web_link":"http://typekit.com/fonts/chalfont"},{"id":"nsmx","__v":0,"browse_info":{"capitals":[],"classification":[],"contrast":[],"language":["ca","de","en","es","fr","it","nl","pt","sv"],"number_style":[],"recommended_for":[],"weight":[],"width":[],"x_height":[]},"description":null,"foundry":"Alan Meeks","lastModified":1588840819580,"name":"Chalky","variations":["n4"],"web_link":"http://typekit.com/fonts/chalky"},{"id":"cztv","__v":0,"browse_info":{"capitals":["uppercase-lowercase"],"classification":["decorative"],"contrast":["regular"],"language":["ca","cs","de","en","es","fr","hu","it","nl","pl","pt","sl","sv"],"number_style":["lowercase"],"recommended_for":[],"weight":["regular"],"width":["regular"],"x_height":["high"]},"description":"Where the heck does a name like Chaloops come from? You know that Chihuahua that used to sell the tacos? Chank’s mother-in-law calls him Chalupa. And the American pluralization for that must be Chaloops, because that’s her nickname for her two little spoiled fuzzball dogs.\r\nAnother comic variation on Chank’s whimsical handwriting, Chaloops is bouncy, quirky, and light-hearted like the Chauncy fonts. But Chaloops has more squiggles and its stroke terminals are mostly square. Just like you.\r\n\r\nChaloops the font comes with a few alternate characters to give your designs a more authentic hand-drawn look. Happy and playful like a pair of frolicsome puppies, this font is perfect for kids’ products and marketing.","foundry":"Chank Co","lastModified":1588840819558,"name":"Chaloops","variations":["n4","n6","n7"],"web_link":"http://typekit.com/fonts/chaloops"},{"id":"gzxl","__v":0,"browse_info":{"capitals":[],"classification":[],"contrast":[],"language":[],"number_style":[],"recommended_for":[],"weight":[],"width":[],"x_height":[]},"description":null,"foundry":"Vintage Voyage Design","is_personal":false,"lastModified":1790380298669,"name":"Chameleon","variations":["n4"],"web_link":"http://fonts.adobe.com/fonts/chameleon"},{"id":"nrry","__v":0,"browse_info":{"capitals":[],"classification":[],"contrast":[],"language":[],"number_style":[],"recommended_for":[],"weight":[],"width":[],"x_height":[]},"description":null,"foundry":"Ludlow Typefaces","is_personal":false,"lastModified":1752277881584,"name":"Chamfer Gothic Ludlow","variations":["n3","n5","n7"],"web_link":"http://fonts.adobe.com/fonts/chamfer-gothic-ludlow"},{"id":"byvt","__v":0,"browse_info":{"capitals":["uppercase-lowercase"],"classification":["decorative"],"contrast":[],"language":["ca","de","en","es","fr","it","nl","pt","sv"],"number_style":["lowercase"],"recommended_for":[],"weight":["heavy","light","regular"],"width":["regular"],"x_height":["high"]},"description":"One of the first messy typewriter fonts to hit the market, Chandler 42 was compiled with forensic care from the voluminous output of a circa 1942 portable.\r\n\r\nAll the eccentric personality of this particular machine is intact: a slightly angled “m,” filled in gaps in the most-used characters; even the number “4” is reminiscent of gasoline pump numbers of its day.\r\nChandler 42 features edges meticulously redrafted by hand, fully developed Western character sets and is available in 4 weights, plus obliques.\r\n\r\nChandler 42 is everything you need to type a 1940s business letter, prep your own vintage facilities report or write that hard boiled novel you've been planning to start.","foundry":"Steve Mehallo","lastModified":1588840819571,"name":"Chandler 42 ","variations":["n3","i3","n4","i4","n5","i5","n8","i8"],"web_link":"http://typekit.com/fonts/chandler-42"},{"id":"jyqq","__v":0,"browse_info":{"capitals":[],"classification":[],"contrast":[],"language":[],"number_style":[],"recommended_for":[],"weight":[],"width":[],"x_height":[]},"description":null,"foundry":"MACHALSKI","lastModified":1695949890450,"name":"Change","variations":["n4","i4","n5","i5","n6","i6","n7","i7"],"web_link":"http://typekit.com/fonts/change"},{"id":"dmwg","__v":0,"browse_info":{"capitals":["uppercase-lowercase"],"classification":["decorative"],"contrast":["low"],"language":["ca","cs","de","en","es","fr","hu","it","mt","nl","pl","pt","ru","sl","sv","tr"],"number_style":["uppercase"],"recommended_for":[],"weight":["heavy","regular"],"width":["extended"],"x_height":["high"]},"description":"Changeling Neo (2008) picks up where Changeling (2003) left off, adding even more alternate characters, including high-waisted variants of f, P, and R; alternate r, y, G, Q, and Y (with forward- and back-slanted variants for the alternate y); alternate k, K, x, and X with more conventional forms; tabular figures; and even support for fractions, superscripts and subscripts. The alternate characters are available through the magic of OpenType, instead of hijacking locations meant for other characters (all of which have been added back in). If all this weren't enough, Changeling Neo has extensive language support for a wide range of Latin-based languages. Kerning has been completely revamped to give Changeling Neo perfect Seventies-style “tight but not touching” spacing.\r\n\r\nChangeling Neo comes in five styles: Light, Regular, Bold, Stencil, and Inline.","foundry":"Mark Simonson Studio","lastModified":1588840819569,"name":"Changeling Neo","variations":["n3","n4","n7"],"web_link":"http://typekit.com/fonts/changeling-neo"},{"id":"tmbb","__v":0,"browse_info":{"capitals":["uppercase-lowercase"],"classification":["decorative"],"contrast":[],"language":["ca","cs","de","en","es","fr","hu","it","mt","nl","pl","pt","ru","sl","sv","tr"],"number_style":["uppercase"],"recommended_for":[],"weight":[],"width":["extended"],"x_height":[]},"description":"Changeling Neo (2008) picks up where Changeling (2003) left off. Changeling Neo has extensive language support for a wide range of Latin-based languages. Kerning has been completely revamped to give Changeling Neo perfect Seventies-style “tight but not touching” spacing. Changeling Neo comes in five styles: Light, Regular, Bold, Stencil, and Inline.\r\n","foundry":"Mark Simonson Studio","lastModified":1588840819583,"name":"Changeling Neo Inline ","variations":["n7"],"web_link":"http://typekit.com/fonts/changeling-neo-inline"},{"id":"gncv","__v":0,"browse_info":{"capitals":["uppercase-lowercase"],"classification":["decorative"],"contrast":[],"language":["ca","cs","de","en","es","fr","hu","it","mt","nl","pl","pt","ru","sl","sv","tr"],"number_style":["uppercase"],"recommended_for":[],"weight":[],"width":["extend