 to allow even tighter spacing. This is not a text font. Coolvetica is a pure display font, intended for big, funky headings and titles. Coolvetica has class based kerning, math symbols, fractions and numeric ordinals.","foundry":"Typodermic","lastModified":1588840819930,"name":"Coolvetica","variations":["n1","i1","n2","i2","n3","i3","n4","i4","n5","i5","n7","i7","n9","i9"],"web_link":"http://typekit.com/fonts/coolvetica"},{"id":"pbzd","__v":0,"browse_info":{"capitals":["uppercase-lowercase"],"classification":["serif"],"contrast":["regular"],"language":["ca","de","en","es","fr","it","nl","pt","sv"],"number_style":["lowercase"],"recommended_for":["headings"],"weight":["heavy"],"width":["regular"],"x_height":["high"]},"description":"Cooper Black is a very heavy version of Cooper Oldstyle (also known simply as Cooper), an innovative typeface with rounded serifs and long ascenders designed in 1919. The Cooper family was the work of Oswald Bruce Cooper, co-owner of the Bertsch & Cooper design firm in Chicago. Cooper Black was first released by the Barnhart Brothers & Spindler foundry of Chicago in 1922. Oz Cooper was fond of saying that the Black fit the needs of “far-sighted printers with near-sighted customers.”","foundry":"Adobe Originals","lastModified":1740095523034,"name":"Cooper Black Std","variations":["n4","i4"],"web_link":"http://fonts.adobe.com/fonts/cooper-black-std"},{"id":"hhbr","__v":0,"browse_info":{"capitals":[],"classification":[],"contrast":[],"language":[],"number_style":[],"recommended_for":[],"weight":[],"width":[],"x_height":[]},"description":null,"foundry":"Process Type Foundry","lastModified":1645751470378,"name":"Coordinates","variations":["n3","i3","n4","i4","n5","i5","n6","i6","n7","i7"],"web_link":"http://typekit.com/fonts/coordinates"},{"id":"stgg","__v":0,"browse_info":{"capitals":[],"classification":[],"contrast":[],"language":["ca","de","en","es","fr","it","nl","pt","sv"],"number_style":[],"recommended_for":[],"weight":[],"width":[],"x_he