,"number_style":["uppercase"],"recommended_for":["headings"],"weight":["light","regular"],"width":["regular"],"x_height":["low"]},"description":"Coquette, designed in 2001, could be the result of a happy marriage of Kabel and French Script. According to Mark, \"The idea for this typeface got stuck in my head in the early ’90s. To me, it feels like it has always existed, even though I’ve never seen anything quite like it. I am happy to offer this unique yet strangely familiar script.\"","foundry":"Mark Simonson Studio","lastModified":1588840819944,"name":"Coquette","variations":["n2","n3","n4","n7","n8","n9"],"web_link":"http://typekit.com/fonts/coquette"},{"id":"zjnv","__v":0,"browse_info":{"capitals":[],"classification":[],"contrast":[],"language":[],"number_style":[],"recommended_for":[],"weight":[],"width":[],"x_height":[]},"description":null,"foundry":"Dalton Maag","is_personal":false,"lastModified":1773791488432,"name":"Coquito","variations":["n4"],"web_link":"http://fonts.adobe.com/fonts/coquito"},{"id":"cklb","__v":0,"browse_info":{"capitals":["uppercase-lowercase"],"classification":["sans-serif"],"contrast":["regular"],"language":["ca","cs","de","en","es","fr","hu","it","mt","nl","pl","pt","sl","sv","tr"],"number_style":["uppercase"],"recommended_for":[],"weight":["regular"],"width":["regular"],"x_height":["high"]},"description":"Cora, designed by Bart Blubaugh, is a sans serif with an experimental bent, offering a large x-height, some contrast of stroke weight, and capitals inspired by classical lettering. The uppercase exhibits classical proportions found in ancient Roman inscriptions, which provides opportunities for setting titles in all caps. \r\nThe large x-height gives it a voice with a little more volume so that those in the back of the room have no trouble hearing. Because the letters seem slightly large, Cora remains clear at smaller point sizes. \r\nIt is a typeface intended to perform well on screen without loosing its attraction in print and the nature of its shapes allows for condensation or expansion without becoming severely distorted. \r\nCora was selected as winner of the Granshan competition 2008 in the text type category.\r\n","foundry":"TypeTogether","lastModified":1588840819939,"name":"Cora","variations":["n4","i4","n5","i5","n7","i7"],"web_link":"http://typekit.com/fonts/cora-web"},{"id":"msyk","__v":0,"browse_info":{"capitals":[],"classification":[],"contrast":[],"language":[],"number_style":[],"recommended_for":[],"weight":[],"width":[],"x_height":[]},"description":null,"foundry":"Google","is_personal":false,"lastModified":1765497087291,"name":"Coral Pixels","variations":["n4"],"web_link":"http://fonts.adobe.com/fonts/coral-pixels"},{"id":"ktbv","__v":0,"browse_info":{"capitals":["uppercase-lowercase"],"classification":["serif"],"contrast":["high"],"language":["ca","cs","de","en","es","fr","hu","it","mt","nl","pl","pt","sl","sv","tr"],"number_style":["lowercase"],"recommended_for":[],"weight":["regular"],"width":["regular"],"x_height":["regular"]},"description":"Originally based on Gerard Unger’s typeface Paradox, Coranto 2 has a larger x-height and in many places has been made more robust. This elegant text newsface creates an ambiance of precision and refinement.","foundry":"TypeTogether","lastModified":1588840819953,"name":"Coranto 2","variations":["n4","i4","n7","i7"],"web_link":"http://typekit.com/fonts/coranto-2"},{"id":"dpvc","__v":0,"browse_info":{"capitals":["uppercase-lowercase"],"classification":["serif"],"contrast":["regular"],"language":["ca","cs","de","en","es","fr","hu","it","mt","nl","pl","pt","sl","sv","tr"],"number_style":["uppercase"],"recommended_for":[],"weight":["heavy","regular"],"width":["regular"],"x_height":["high"]},"description":null,"foundry":"TypeTogether","lastModified":1588840819941,"name":"Coranto 2 Headline","variations":["n3","n4","n6","n7"],"web_link":"http://typekit.com/fonts/coranto-2-headline"},{"id":"dsqh","__v":0,"browse_info":{"capitals":[],"classificat